rship in the Roman Republic", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "gnaeus-cornelius-scipio-hispanus", "targetName": "Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio Hispanus", "context": "The established institution of the Roman censorship provided the framework and authority for his significant actions as censor in 204 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "gnaeus-cornelius-scipio-hispanus", "sourceName": "Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio Hispanus", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "senatorial-expulsions-204-bce", "targetName": "Senatorial Expulsions of 204 BCE", "context": "As censor, he was instrumental in the expulsion of seven senators, including Quintus Fulvius Flaccus, in 204 BCE."}], "places": [{"name": "Rome, Roman Republic", "role": "Political center"}, {"name": "Sardinia, Roman Republic", "role": "Praetorship province"}, {"name": "Italian Peninsula, Roman Republic", "role": "Sphere of influence"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio Hispanus", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "gnaeus-cornelius-scipio-hispanus"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1533120", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gnaeus_Cornelius_Scipio_Hispanus"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio Hispanus's censorship in 204 BCE significantly reinforced the moral authority of the Roman censorial office, setting precedents for senatorial discipline and contributing to the Republic's internal stability post-Second Punic War.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-13T07:29:50Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27460354357", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio Hispanus of the Scipiones branch of the gens Cornelia, was a Roman politician.", "newValue": "Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio Hispanus, a prominent figure from the distinguished Scipiones branch of the Roman gens Cornelia, served as a Roman politician during the tumultuous period of the Second Punic War and its immediate aftermath. Born at an unknown date and dying in 200 BCE, his career is notable for his tenure as censor in 204 BCE, a magistracy that wielded immense moral and political authority within the Roman Republic. His actions reflected the internal dynamics of Roman society as it consolidated its power and sought to maintain civic virtue amidst profound external challenges.

His political career saw him ascend through the customary sequence of offices, known as the *cursus honorum*. He held the praetorship in 215 BCE, potentially in Sardinia, and later achieved the pinnacle of his public service as censor in 204 BCE. This powerful office, shared with his colleague Publius Licinius Crassus Dives, empowered him to assess property for taxation, revise the citizen rolls, and, crucially, to regulate public morals (*regimen morum*). During his censorship, he notably expelled seven senators, including Quintus Fulvius Flaccus, an act that underscored the censors' formidable power to maintain the *dignitas* and integrity of the senatorial order, even against established figures.

The censorship of Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio Hispanus contributed significantly to the evolving definition of senatorial conduct and the moral authority of the Roman state during a critical juncture. His actions reinforced the censors' role as guardians of the *mos maiorum* (ancestral custom) and set important precedents for the exercise of censorial power in future generations. This period saw Rome grappling with the moral and social consequences of prolonged warfare, and the censors' interventions were vital in reaffirming republican values and social order, thereby contributing to the Republic's internal stability as it emerged victorious from its conflict with Carthage."}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-13T07:29:50Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27460354357", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "200 BCE, Ancient Rome"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "200 BCE, Ancient Rome"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing Second Punic War necessitated strong leadership and moral oversight within the Roman Republic's political structure.", "The established tradition of the Roman Republic's magistracies, particularly the powerful censorship, allowed for such influential actions.", "The socio-political landscape of late 3rd century BCE Rome, marked by elite competition and the need to reinforce civic virtue, created an environment for his rise."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-13T07:29:50Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27460354357", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "200 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "200 BCE"}], "newValue": ["His censorship reinforced the authority